## 3. Soft scoring — only runs on candidates that already passed every gate above

Corrected priority order, straight from the meeting feedback ("the top one needs to be trained in the assessment... family preference doesn't matter if the people they prefer can't do the assessment"):

| Rank | Factor | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Family preference / continuity** (set at consent visit; "knows the family, did the last visit") | Highest-weighted soft factor — but only among candidates who already cleared every hard gate. |
| 2 | **Availability freshness** | Prefer candidates with more recently synced, higher-confidence evidence over stale-but-technically-clear evidence. |
| 3 | **Out-of-hours equity** | Penalize candidates with an above-average recent out-of-hours tally, to keep evening/weekend load distributed fairly, per the manual's Slack-canvas tracking method being replaced by a real column. |
| 4 | **Vehicle optimization** | Prefer van-trained pairs for short drives, preserve van-trained staff for concurrent short trips, no penalty for rental-car-only pairs on long drives. |
| 5 | **Skill-scarcity** | Slight penalty for assigning a highly versatile coordinator (e.g., Sanjana, reliable across 1–12m) to a visit that a narrower-scope coordinator (e.g., Makenzie, 6–12m specific) could equally cover — keeps versatile staff free for visits only they can do. |

Every ranked result must render its **reason string** in the UI — e.g., "Recommended: family's coordinator from last visit; van-trained; no out-of-hours conflict" — matching the live site's own framing of "explains who it would send." A result with `insufficient evidence` from §2 rule 6 must render distinctly (e.g., "Sync calendars first — no current data for Lauren on this date") rather than being silently omitted, so the scheduler always knows *why* someone isn't ranked.

